WEBVTT

00:00:01.578 --> 00:00:12.118
Valverde valley, one in the afternoon. A wildfire has broken out on Pine Hill, and a strong north-east wind is pushing it towards the village of Pineta.

00:00:12.169 --> 00:00:22.629
Four people run the response: the exercise director, the mayor of Valverde, the fire commander, and a regional civil protection officer who works in Armenian.

00:00:22.682 --> 00:00:31.622
The exercise begins. The fire already covers five hectares and is spreading south-west through dry pine forest.

00:00:31.675 --> 00:00:41.815
The first reports come in: emergency calls about the fire, a warning that the primary school is in session, and a fire-behaviour estimate for the commander.

00:00:41.866 --> 00:00:50.766
In Pineta, 120 pupils and staff are still inside the primary school, and the school has no transport of its own.

00:00:50.818 --> 00:00:59.958
The mayor acts at once: warn the village, evacuate the school to the sports centre in Valverde, and close the northern road.

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:09.030
The fire commander sends ground crews to cut fire breaks north and east of the village, and an air tanker to wet the forest above it.

00:01:09.641 --> 00:01:18.021
Before anything happens on the ground, every order is checked against the people, vehicles and authority actually available.

00:01:18.073 --> 00:01:24.933
A problem emerges: evacuating the school needs 3 buses, and the town has only 2.

00:01:24.984 --> 00:01:31.164
Exercise control steps in: the regional transport company can release one more bus.

00:01:31.241 --> 00:01:36.501
The extra bus is confirmed, and the mayor is told it is on its way.

00:01:36.552 --> 00:01:41.932
The fire commander's plan goes ahead: two fire breaks and a water drop.

00:01:41.983 --> 00:01:49.043
With three buses available, the school evacuation can start, together with a warning to the whole village.

00:01:49.094 --> 00:01:53.714
The mayor receives the confirmation, and the buses head for the school.

00:01:56.225 --> 00:02:04.205
The next thirty minutes unfold. The fire runs downhill towards Pineta while the crews race to finish the breaks.

00:02:04.257 --> 00:02:15.477
The tallest flames mark where the fire burns hottest and where most simulated runs agree. The faint glow ahead shows where it could spread next.

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:24.790
The fire breaks hold at the edge of the village, and the children reach the sports centre safely. The northern road is lost to the flames.

00:02:24.842 --> 00:02:29.542
Each responder receives a report on what has changed on the ground.

00:02:32.072 --> 00:02:41.532
Half past one. The wind strengthens to 55 kilometres per hour, and a second fire starts in the farmland south-east of Pineta.

00:02:41.584 --> 00:02:55.044
Local resources are stretched. Regional civil protection asks for international help: two water-bombing aircraft through the EU Civil Protection Mechanism, and two more ground crews.

00:02:55.096 --> 00:02:59.556
Under pressure, the fire commander sends an unclear order…

00:02:59.609 --> 00:03:04.909
…and is asked exactly where the crews should go, instead of anyone guessing.

00:03:04.961 --> 00:03:10.541
The answer comes back: the eastern edge of the village, and the slope above it.

00:03:10.593 --> 00:03:17.413
The request for aircraft and the new fire breaks are approved; the unclear order is sent back.

00:03:25.957 --> 00:03:37.457
The next hour. Reinforcements arrive and the breaks keep the fire out of Pineta, but the second fire races through the farmland, where nobody was warned.

00:03:58.931 --> 00:04:13.191
By half past two, Pineta is safe and every child is accounted for, but farmhouses have burned and the aircraft are still on their way. Every decision and its consequences are recorded for the debrief.

